[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Tutorial: Spies and Partial Mocking in Mockito"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Mockito, a mock usually creates a brand new instance of a class where all methods are stubbed by default and return null, false, or zero. However, there are scenarios where you want to test only a specific part of a real object while keeping the rest of the actual implementation intact. This is known as partial mocking, and it is achieved using Mockito Spies."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"What is a Spy?"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"A spy wraps a real object. When you use a spy, all the normal methods of the real object will be invoked unless they are explicitly stubbed. Spies are particularly useful when dealing with legacy code or when you need to verify interactions on a real working object while overriding only one or two problematic methods."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Creating a Spy"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can create a spy in Mockito using the @Spy annotation or the Mockito.spy() method. Here is an example of using the method approach:"},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"List<String> list = new ArrayList<>();
List<String> spyList = Mockito.spy(list);"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Alternatively, using annotations requires initializing Mockito mocks, typically done in a setup method or via MockitoAnnotations.openMocks(this):"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"@Spy
private List<String> annotatedSpyList = new ArrayList<>();"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Stubbing a Spy: The Pitfall"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Stubbing a spy is different from stubbing a standard mock. If you use the standard when(spy.method()).thenReturn(value) syntax, Mockito will actually execute the real method first before attempting to stub it. If the real method relies on uninitialized dependencies, this can throw a NullPointerException."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Using doReturn for Safe Stubbing"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"To avoid executing the real method during stubbing, you must use the doReturn().when() syntax. This tells Mockito what value to return without triggering the actual method execution."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"List<String> spyList = Mockito.spy(new ArrayList<>());

// Correct way to stub a spy
Mockito.doReturn("Stubbed Value").when(spyList).get(0);

// This will return "Stubbed Value" without calling the real get(0) method
System.out.println(spyList.get(0));"},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Key Differences: Mocks vs. Spies"},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Mocks: By default, all methods return empty/default values. Real methods are never called unless explicitly told to do so."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Spies: By default, real methods are called. You must explicitly stub a method if you want it to return a specific value."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Spies provide a powerful way to perform partial mocking in unit tests. By remembering to use doReturn() instead of when(), you can safely override specific behaviors in real objects while verifying the rest of your application logic."}]
